// WHAT THIS FILE DOES
// The security rules for the Firestore database in the chernozem-danny
// Firebase project. They replace the "test mode" rules, which let anyone
// read and write everything and stopped working after 30 days.
//
// The rule: no request from a browser or a phone is allowed, signed in or
// not. Nothing in a browser uses Firestore, so closing it to every client
// takes nothing away and leaves nothing to attack.
//
// Why not "anyone signed in": anyone can create an account on the
// Register page, and the web config is public by design, so "signed in"
// would only mean "has an account". Any stranger with an account could
// then read, change or delete every field, reading and note.
//
// What uses Firestore today, and why it keeps working:
//   - This app (src/, server/) does not read or write Firestore at all.
//     Accounts use Firebase Authentication, which these rules do not touch.
//   - scripts/fetch-ndvi.js and scripts/seed-fields.js write "fields" and
//     "fields/<id>/readings". They sign in with the service-account key
//     through firebase-admin, which skips these rules, so they keep
//     working.
//   - "notes" holds the notes from the old Notes page. Nothing reads it
//     from a browser any more.
//
// If a page ever needs Firestore from the browser, open only that
// collection, and only as far as it needs (for example each person's own
// document: request.auth.uid == userId), never every document.
//
// WHO EDITS IT
// Danny or William. Deploy a change with:
//   npx firebase-tools deploy --only firestore:rules --project chernozem-danny

rules_version = '2';

service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    // Every document, in every collection and subcollection.
    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
